{"name":"Curlew","hex":"#766859","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"curlew","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Any of several migratory wading birds in the genus Numenius, remarkable for their long, slender, downcurved bills.","A stone curlew (Burhinidae spp.)."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/curlew"}],"entries":[{"title":"Curlew","blocks":[{"text":"The curlews are a group of eight species of birds in the genus Numenius, characterised by their long, slender, downcurved bills and mottled brown plumage. The English name is imitative of the Eurasian curlew's call, but may have been influenced by the Old French corliu, \"messenger\", from courir, \"to run\". It was first recorded in 1377 in Langland's Piers Plowman \"Fissch to lyue in þe flode..Þe corlue by kynde of þe eyre\". In Europe, \"curlew\" usually refers to one species, the Eurasian curlew."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/08/Curlew_-_natures_pics.jpg/330px-Curlew_-_natures_pics.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Curlew"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0c/Whimbrel_Numenius_phaeopus.jpg/500px-Whimbrel_Numenius_phaeopus.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0c/Whimbrel_Numenius_phaeopus.jpg/1280px-Whimbrel_Numenius_phaeopus.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0c/Whimbrel_Numenius_phaeopus.jpg/500px-Whimbrel_Numenius_phaeopus.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AWhimbrel_Numenius_phaeopus.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/68/Numenius_hudsonicus_Zarapito_trinador_Hudsonian_Whimbrel_%281536670490